It's a "tall S" that only looks kind-of like an "f" (if
you look closely, it lacks the cross-bar).  
And it's used only for internal s, the modern short s was 
(usually) used for terminal "s" even when tall s was prevalent.

In Greek, a weird-looking lowercase sigma is used for 
internal sigma, but something that looks like an English s 
is used for terminal sigma.  I don't know whether that's 
related.

Gothic also had the two (tall/short) S forms used the same
way.

I'm using "internal" loosely; the tall/weird-looking form 
_is_ used for leading s/sigma, so I guess "non-terminal" 
would be more accurate.

